WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:06.220
It's award season again and today we're talking about the Golden Globes?

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.300
Why the heck should we care about them?

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.760
Well where else do you get to see the rampant alcoholism of Hollywood?

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.700
Or the positive effects of valium?

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:22.840
Or more alcohol?

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.720
And that's not even counting good old Katniss Everstique!

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.960
But seriously with everyone stoned out of their minds at this thing, this is is SO much more entertaining than the Oscars!

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:36.540
*crickets chirping*

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:57.060
Hello Internet! Welcome to FILM Theory!

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:00.520
Proudly nominated for best speculation in a leading role!

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:01.640
So let's just cut right to the chase.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.960
Award shows can be pretty dumb like

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.340
one award show giving an award to another award show

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:11.940
whose crew is working on the current award show presenting the award levels of dumb.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.880
But hey, I still watch 'em. Because let's be honest:

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.060
What else am I gonna sarcastically live-tweet about on a Sunday night?

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.600
But outside of the enormously rich creating an event that hypes up their own height,

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.520
I also like watching these things to figure out their patterns, break the game.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.460
Like say for instance that one time I did a two-part episode on how to hack the Oscars,

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:46.280
and said that the reason Leonardo DiCaprio didn't win on his first four nominations was that he was too young,

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:50.460
since the vast majority of Best Actor Awards go to men over the age of forty.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:55.240
And wouldn't you know it a year after I uploaded that video Leo happens to pull out the win for The Revenant

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.800
At the ripe old age of forty-one.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:58.700
Nailed it!

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:02.020
You may not have thanked me out loud during your speech or on Twitter

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.600
Or have responded to literally the thousands of tweets you were getting about my video being right Leo,

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.320
But in my heart I knew.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.080
I KNEW.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:12.720
Anyway, those videos were about how to make movies that are Oscar-winners.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.600
Obviously helpful to my pal and loyal theorist, Leo Dicap,

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.520
But probably not to most of you watching.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.700
So this year we're doing something a bit more practical.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.800
Sure you might not walk away with a gold statue but you'll get something even better,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.400
Cash straight out of your boss's pocket.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.160
And that's the sweetest cash of all.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.500
So after a year FULL of bad predictions

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:57.520
Proposing today is that this group in Rogue One turns into THIS group from the sequel trilogy.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:03.340
Let's start 2017 off right by giving you the tools you need...

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.420
to hack the Oscars.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.140
Now when I started doing my homework on this episode,

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:13.600
I expected to talk a lot about how studying the Golden Globes would be the best way to get your Oscars pics in order.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.700
I mean it IS the first major award show of the season

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:21.280
And next to the Oscars, is by far the most-watched of any of the big movie awards.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.040
But just goin' in and plugging the Golden Globe winners into your Oscar pool comes with some big risks.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.920
First, credit where credit is due.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.720
The Golden Globes are really good at picking out Best Actor and Best Actress awards.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.360
In the last twenty years, seventy percent of Best Actor Oscar-winners

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.020
also won a Golden Globe Award for that performance.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.080
For Best Actress, that number shoots up to a whopping ninety percent.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.400
And there are other categories that have a really high correlation between Oscar wins and Golden Globe wins as well.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.380
Like screenplay at eighty percent.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:51.440
Now at first those may seem like huge numbers,

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:52.800
and granted they are,

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.660
but they're also a bit misleading.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:59.260
You see the Academy Awards and Golden Globes don't give out these awards the same way.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:03.160
Sure seventy percent of Best Actors and ninety percent of Best Actresses at the Oscars

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.040
also won themselves a Golden Globe.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.880
But that's because they had TWO shots to do it.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:12.560
The Golden Globe splits its leading actor categories into comedies and dramas.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.800
So while it is highly likely that one of those two actors or actresses is going to win the Academy Award,

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:19.800
how to do you know which of the two?

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.620
Is it a fifty-fifty chance? Flip a coin?

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.440
Pfft. That don't fly here.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:25.840
And for screenplays its the opposite problem.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.800
One Oscar is given for Best Original Screenplay, and one is given for Best Adapted Screenplay.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.240
Whereas the Golden Globes just gives out one for screenplay.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.100
And all those technical awards that make or break your prediction list,

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.560
Like cinematography, sound mixing, film editing?

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:41.620
Not here my friends.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:44.200
And the last problem and probably the biggest one

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.400
is that the Golden Globes have gotten worse in predicting over the years.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:53.180
From 1983 to 2003 one of the Golden Globe film winners for either Best Comedy or Best Drama,

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:56.920
went on to win the Oscar for Best Picture, eighteen out of twenty-one times.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:59.780
It's a very respectable eighty-six percent of the time.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:01.560
In the twelve years since though,

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.980
it's dropped to thirty-three percent.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:06.880
And it's basically making two guesses every year!

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:08.400
That SUCKS.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.580
But it's also a really weird trend right?

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.340
To go from being so good at guessing to performing less than chance?

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:15.940
So what's happening here?

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:20.440
Well, we frankly chose that the taste of the American industry are becoming more and more different

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:22.220
from the tastes of the rest of the world.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:24.920
The Golden Globes are handed out by the Hollywood Foreign Press,

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:28.480
a group of journalists and photographers that reports on the entertainment industry,

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.400
for sites outside of the U.S.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:31.940
The Academy, on the other hand,

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:34.760
consists of about six thousand six hundred people.

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:40.360
Mostly US-based, historically ninety percent white, and all being film industry professionals

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:42.600
with most still being active in making movies.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.260
But it's also worth noting that this year,

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.220
it's six thousand six hundred.

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.480
That extra six-hundred is noteworthy.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.020
Because those six hundred were added just this year

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.420
in response to all the complaints about a lack of diversity in the Academy.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:55.820
Is that enough to sway the votes?

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:56.440
Probably not.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.180
But you are going to start to see things shift away from the old guard

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:03.100
and the Academy probably over-correct for the sins of the last couple years.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:07.260
Anyway tl;dr, the Golden Globes are fun and all to see Jack Nicholson hopped up on valium,

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:09.380
but if you're serious about winning your Oscar pool,

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:11.880
you're gonna have to go a lot deeper than that.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.000
So where does that mean we should turn?

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:14.540
Well.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.340
I looked at the past twenty years of awards handed out

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:23.320
by the Screen Actors' Guild, Writers' Guild of America, Directors' Guild of America, Producers' Guild of America

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:28.460
BAFTA, Satellite Awards, Critics' Choice, National Society of Film Critics, Online Film Critics Society,

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:32.700
New York Film Critics Circle and Los Angeles Film Critics Association. Whoo!

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:36.320
If I never have to write the name "Meryl Streep" into an Excel spreadsheet again,

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:38.060
I will die a happy man.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:40.980
Obviously there was a TON of data to wade through

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:45.800
so I'm gonna give you the cliffnotes and tell you the three biggest lessons learned about Oscar predictibility

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:48.200
from this little data analysis project I did.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:51.160
Number one, trust the guilds.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:54.860
In that laundry list of award shows I just rattled of, you heard four guilds mentioned:

00:06:54.860 --> 00:07:00.860
the Screen Actors' Guild, the Directors' Guild of America, the Producer's Guild of America, and the Writers' Guild of America.

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:03.520
These awards largely go under the radar,

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:04.620
but they shouldn't.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.300
Each one of them was the single best predictor of the awards that they specialize in.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:13.060
The Screen Actors' Guild predicted the Best Actor Award eighty percent of the time

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:15.940
and the Best Actress Award seventy five percent of the time;

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:19.940
the Writers' Guild selected the ultimate winner for Adapted and Original Screenplay

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:21.520
at a rate of seventy percent each;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:25.600
the Directors' Guild picked the Best Director Oscar winner eighty-five percent of the time;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:27.920
-well ahead of ANY of the other award shows-

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.640
and THE most accurate predictor for Best Picture

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:36.420
is an award you probably didn't even know existed, the Producers' Guild of America Award at seventy percent.

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:37.640
This all makes sense right?

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:41.700
They're focused on the American voting block and they're all specialized in their individual categories.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:42.360
Logic.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:45.080
Lesson number two, ignore the critics.

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:49.320
In my data analysis, I mentioned that I included the pics of several critics associations

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:52.960
and ooo-eee Mr. Poopy Butthole was that a waste of time.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:58.180
The New York Film Critics Circle, L.A. Film Critics Association, Online Film Critics Society

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:03.220
and National Society of Film Critics are not only very redundant in what they've named their organizations,

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:07.280
but are also VERY unreliable across the board.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:07.880
NONE!

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:13.680
None of those four award shows pick the Oscar-winner in ANY category more than HALF the time.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:14.740
ANY CATEGORY!

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:17.540
And their Best Picture pics were especially bad.

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:24.040
The Los Angeles Film Critics Society picked the Best Picture Oscar-winner TWICE in the last twenty years.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:24.540
Seriously.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:28.420
We must have some bitter failed filmmakers in the L.A. Film Critics Society

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:30.880
because with a ten percent accuracy rate,

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:33.800
it seems they are intentionally trying to go against the grain!

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:36.000
WHOOP! WHOOP! WHOOP!

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.700
We got some hipsters over here! Hipster Alert!

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.760
That said there is one exception.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.280
The Critics' Choice Awards are actually surprisingly good

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:46.920
at picking Best Cinematography, which it's done eighty five percent of the time,

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:49.640
and Best Original Screenplay, which it only missed once

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:52.280
in the eleven times that it's handed out the award.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:54.180
So when it comes to those more obscure categories

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:55.700
that's a good one to reference.

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:59.800
Which brings us to lesson number three: Award shows themselves aren't enough.

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:02.420
Sure seventy percent accuracy rating for a given category

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:06.200
is pretty good, but as any of you who watch our GTLIVE livestreams know:

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:07.440
I'm not a big fan of losing.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:11.940
NO! I HIT THE DOT! I HIT IT! I HIT IT!

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:13.820
And I want YOU to be confident!

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:17.620
That you're finally gonna get some bragging rights over Bill from accounting.

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:19.480
He is so smug.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:22.340
Screw you Bill! You and your... accounting!

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:24.700
Whatever accountants have. A computer!

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:28.160
Gah! That's too general... Your calculator! Yeah!

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:29.880
That got him, got him good.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:31.360
But seriously you guys can relate right?

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:35.520
Every office, school, classroom whatever has that, like, one film hipster.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:39.540
Oh! I can't believe that they snubbed this obscure Iranian foreign film!

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:40.260
Well screw them!

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:41.360
This is our time to shine.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:44.560
Today, I set out to give you THE best way to make your predictions.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:48.380
The best odds to have the ability to rub it in those film hipster faces

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:50.500
and dang it I intend to deliver!

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:55.320
Luckily the best answer to this problem is the answer that I use for practically every problem these days:

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:57.000
the Internet.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.660
That may seem vague but stick with me.

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:00.120
The digital age has really changed

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:03.580
the amount of data that we can collect on what people are thinking, and as a result

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:06.680
the likeliest outcomes of things that people have to decide.

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:09.740
If you're just looking for other award shows and how predictive THEY are,

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:13.860
then you only really have a few data points to go off of for predicting any one award.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:17.780
But some analysts are starting to create new models to predict the Academy Awards

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:21.700
that consider thousands, if not millions, of data points.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:25.320
Take, for instance, my favorite short form sarcasm generator and meme filter:

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:25.960
Twitter.

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:30.540
Last year, a data scientist named Paul Singman ventured to make a model that predicted the Oscars

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:34.740
based off the volume of Twitter mentions and rankings of how positive they were.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:35.960
Sure it wasn't perfect,

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:38.240
but Singman's model got a lot of the awards right.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:42.980
Like Best Actor, Best Actress, Best Director, Best Original Score and Best Animated Feature.

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:45.080
And that was the models first ever test try.

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:49.020
With more results added to the model to see what it got right and what it got wrong from last year,

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:52.840
it's highly likely that his model this year is gonna be even more accurate.

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:56.820
Still, even Singman's model missed what was probably the biggest upset of last year:

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:57.760
Best Picture.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:01.060
Picking The Revenant over the actual winner of Spotlight.

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:03.600
But seriously, everyone missed that one.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:08.700
In fact, Spotlight actually became the first film in over sixty years to win Best Picture

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:10.880
while only winning one other award.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:15.240
In analyzing all these different strategies, it was the biggest outlaw trying to account for.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:17.260
How do you predict the unpredictable?

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:20.920
I mean seriously, did ANYBODY see that win for Spotlight coming?

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:24.200
Well yes, but not someone, something.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:28.060
It took a lot of research and a lot of trial-and-error but I finally found it.

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:29.860
Skynet. I mean sorry, Google.

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:31.280
Google. Google's what I meant.

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:36.060
A quick look at the Google trends data for the search terms The Revenant, The Big Short and Spotlight

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:39.620
starting at the beginning of 2016, shows us an interesting pattern.

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:44.660
In all of those graphs we see a big spike in the week of February 28th and that's to be expected,

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:46.640
since that's when the Oscars were handed out.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:51.660
But it's the shape of the graph in January and early February that's the most interesting.

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:55.940
Both The Revenant and The Big Short came into 2016 with very hot search traffic,

00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:59.560
but then cooled down significantly the couple of weeks leading up to the Oscars.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:04.120
Spotlight, on the other hand, started very flat but gradually grew,

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:08.500
consistently growing in search traffic through the first six weeks of 2016.

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:12.900
It's plausible that these models of search traffic mirror the behavior of the Academy voters.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:18.820
If voters for the Oscars checked out The Big Short and The Revenant while they were hot in search traffic in early January,

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:25.820
it's likely that they were watching films that recieved fewer accolades, like Spotlight, a bit closer to they time that they were actually voting.

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:28.540
The couple of weeks right before the big awards.

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:32.340
Thereby making them subject to our good old friend, the recency effect.

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:36.580
A psychological phenomenon which states that we are more likely to remember and prefer things

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:39.380
that we have seen or watched more recently.

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:43.020
So while The Big Short and The Revenant were distant memories in the voters' minds

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:44.500
having just seen Spotlight,

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:47.360
that's the one they vote for.

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:51.760
Put simply, you don't want your movie to be losing steam as it heads into the Oscars.

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:56.120
Because the two weeks before the ceremony, is when the voting really happens.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:57.740
So here we are, this is it.

00:12:57.740 --> 00:12:58.740
This is the moment.

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:03.220
Bill from accounting is headed down the hall, waving that Oscar valet

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:06.340
in his grubby little hands, looking as cocky as always.

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:09.660
Thoughts of that Iranian documentary running through his head.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:11.860
What are you going to do to wipe that grin off his face?

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:14.920
Let me tell ya: step one, follow the guilds.

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:18.200
Look up the winners from each respective guild and mark them off.

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:21.000
Add in some of the categories that we mentioned from the Critics' Choice.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:24.360
Step two: double-check your choices with Twitter trends.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:28.060
And step three: before you turn in your ballet the Friday before the Oscars,

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:32.020
double-check Google trends to see if any underdogs have been gaining in search.

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:37.360
And finally step four: watch the ceremony and celebrate as the points come rolling in.

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:42.340
And when Monday morning rolls around, rub your astute choice of best foreign film documentary

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:43.380
in Bill's face.

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:48.240
Because at the end of the day, who cares which multi-millionare walked away with a golden statue?

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:52.480
All that matters is you, winning bragging rights for the next year.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:56.060
SCREW YOU BILL! You and your calculator!

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:59.340
Just because it has subtitles doesn't make it good.

